.\" sysctl.3 - sysctl library function
.TH SYSCTL 3 "March 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
sysctl, sysctl_int, sysctl_uint, sysctl_quad, sysctl_string, sysctl_get_buf \- get or set system information
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysctl.h>
.PP
.BI "int sysctl(int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", void *" oldp ", size_t *" oldlenp ", void *" newp ", size_t " newlen ");"
.PP
.BI "int sysctl_int(const int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", int *" oldp ", int *" newp ");"
.PP
.BI "int sysctl_uint(const int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", unsigned int *" oldp ", unsigned int *" newp ");"
.PP
.BI "int sysctl_quad(const int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", uint64_t *" oldp ", uint64_t *" newp ");"
.PP
.BI "int sysctl_string(const int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", char *" oldp ", size_t *" oldlenp ", const char *" newp ");"
.PP
.BI "void *sysctl_get_buf(const int *" name ", unsigned int " namelen ", size_t *" lenp ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR sysctl ()
function retrieves or sets kernel state via a Management Information Base (MIB) path.
The
.I name
argument is an array of
.I namelen
integers identifying the parameter.
If
.I oldp
is non-NULL, the current value is copied there and
.I *oldlenp
is updated with the actual size.
If
.I newp
is non-NULL, the value is set from the
.I newlen
bytes at that address.
.PP
The typed convenience helpers
.RB ( sysctl_int (),
.BR sysctl_uint (),
.BR sysctl_quad ())
perform size validation automatically, returning
.B EINVAL
if the kernel returns a size that does not match the expected type.
.PP
.BR sysctl_string ()
handles NUL-terminated string parameters.
.PP
.BR sysctl_get_buf ()
probes the required size, allocates a buffer, and retrieves the data
in a retry loop (up to 5 attempts) to handle concurrent size changes.
The caller must
.BR free (3)
the returned buffer.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sysctl ()
and the typed helpers return 0 on success, or \-1 on error (with
.I errno
set).
.PP
.BR sysctl_get_buf ()
returns a pointer to the allocated buffer on success, or NULL on error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
Invalid MIB path, namelen out of range [1, CTL_MAXNAME], or type size mismatch.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Insufficient memory for buffer allocation.
.TP
.B ENOENT
The named parameter does not exist.
.SH NOTES
All functions are thread-safe and reentrant.
No static mutable state is used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sysctlbyname (3),
.BR sysctlnametomib (3),
.BR sysctl (2)
